## Verification

Verified the LVM configuration using:

```bash
sudo pvs
sudo vgs
sudo lvs
sudo vgdisplay
```

These commands confirmed that the Physical Volume, Volume Group, and both Logical Volumes were created successfully.

---

## Lessons Learned

This lab introduced the basic workflow of Logical Volume Manager (LVM). I learned that a Physical Volume can be created directly on an entire disk without first creating a partition. I also learned how Volume Groups combine available storage into a storage pool, allowing multiple Logical Volumes to be created from the available space.

---

## Real-World Application

LVM is commonly used on enterprise Linux servers because it provides flexible storage management. Administrators can allocate storage, create additional Logical Volumes, and expand existing storage without being constrained by traditional partition layouts.

---

## Key Takeaways

- Identified available block devices using `lsblk`.
- Initialized an entire disk as a Physical Volume.
- Created a Volume Group using an 8 MiB physical extent size.
- Created two Logical Volumes from the Volume Group.
- Verified each stage of the LVM configuration using `pvs`, `vgs`, `lvs`, and `vgdisplay`.
